  "notes": [
    "Reliability is the real win: with-skill pass-rate variance is +/-5% vs baseline +/-23%. Two baselines invented non-portable schemas (flat yaw, 'target'/'image' field names, nested under 'tour') scoring 55-73%; the one baseline at 100% only got there by discovering the repo's gold tour.json + grader and reverse-engineering them (contaminated). On a fresh property with no examples, baseline would reliably miss the backend schema.",
    "The single with-skill miss (eval-1, 10/11) is the bidirectionality assertion - a DELIBERATE one-way entrance->bathroom the agent made under the skill's 'one-way tolerable if reachable' clause. Tightening the skill to always-reciprocal makes it 11/11 and matches the gold tours (00101/00173 are fully bidirectional).",
    "Discriminating assertions are the schema ones (image_url resolves, target_scene_id resolves, initial_scene_id exists, narration_script present). Range/dedup assertions passed for nearly all runs - baselines passed them vacuously (no valid hotspots -> nothing out of range), so they don't separate skill from baseline.",
    "Cost: skill is ~4x slower (572s vs 138s) and ~2x tokens (108k vs 52k), entirely from per-image vision analysis + overlay verification + fix iterations. Acceptable for a build-once authoring task where correctness/portability matter.",
    "UNANIMOUS skill friction (all 4 skill agents): overlay --out is joined onto property_dir so relative paths leak into seed_properties/.claude/...; pitch-band numbers disagree (SKILL says -25..-40, script warns -45..-18) and far/open-plan doorways compute above -18; and 'openings outnumber panoramas' needs an explicit 'drop dangling openings' rule. These are the iteration-2 fixes."
  ]
